WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.861 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The starter doubles five input values.

00:00:03.081 --> 00:00:05.919 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Follow the third value through one GPU thread,

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:10.085 line:0 position:50% size:95%
and distinguish that logical thread from the hardware that executes it.

00:00:12.105 --> 00:00:14.345 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The starter chooses four threads per block.

00:00:14.565 --> 00:00:18.192 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Five elements require two blocks, so eight threads are launched.

00:00:18.412 --> 00:00:21.100 line:0 position:50% size:95%
A block groups threads; it is not an SM,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.413 line:0 position:50% size:95%
and a thread is not a dedicated arithmetic unit.

00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:29.313 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Each block runs on one streaming multiprocessor.

00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:32.968 line:0 position:50% size:95%
This illustration places both blocks on the same SM.

00:00:33.188 --> 00:00:36.729 line:0 position:50% size:95%
They could instead be assigned to different SMs;

00:00:36.949 --> 00:00:41.472 line:0 position:50% size:95%
the launch does not choose their placement or promise simultaneous execution.

00:00:43.492 --> 00:00:46.799 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Threads from one block form warps with thirty-two positions.

00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:50.368 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Each block here occupies four positions in its own warp.

00:00:50.588 --> 00:00:55.153 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The other positions contain no launched threads; another block cannot fill them.

00:00:57.173 --> 00:01:00.288 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Select block zero. Follow its local thread two.

00:01:00.508 --> 00:01:02.961 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Its index is zero times four plus two.

00:01:03.181 --> 00:01:06.317 line:0 position:50% size:95%
It selects input index two and output index two.

00:01:06.537 --> 00:01:09.929 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The index chooses data, not a physical arithmetic unit.

00:01:11.949 --> 00:01:16.515 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The selected thread requests the value at input index two: three point zero.

00:01:16.735 --> 00:01:21.023 line:0 position:50% size:95%
On the SM, registers hold thread working values for instructions.

00:01:21.243 --> 00:01:23.312 line:0 position:50% size:95%
This drawing shows the dependency,

00:01:23.532 --> 00:01:27.159 line:0 position:50% size:95%
not an exact register allocation or cache transaction.

00:01:29.179 --> 00:01:33.403 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The multiply uses that thread's value and produces six point zero.

00:01:33.623 --> 00:01:38.657 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Threads retain their own working values while sharing the SM's execution hardware.

00:01:38.877 --> 00:01:43.933 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Separate thread values do not require a separate permanent processor for every thread.

00:01:45.953 --> 00:01:49.751 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The store sends the result to output index two in device storage.

00:01:49.971 --> 00:01:52.595 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Other valid threads use their own indices.

00:01:52.815 --> 00:01:55.631 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The matching positions come from the index expression,

00:01:55.851 --> 00:01:58.176 line:0 position:50% size:95%
not the order in which threads happen to finish.

00:02:00.196 --> 00:02:03.097 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Block one's first thread handles the fifth element.

00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:05.045 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Its other three threads exist,

00:02:05.265 --> 00:02:08.103 line:0 position:50% size:95%
but the bounds check skips their array reads and writes.

00:02:08.323 --> 00:02:11.523 line:0 position:50% size:95%
These are different from the twenty-eight unused warp positions,

00:02:11.743 --> 00:02:13.300 line:0 position:50% size:95%
which contain no threads.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.011 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Pause and predict: with a multiplier of zero point five,

00:02:19.231 --> 00:02:21.215 line:0 position:50% size:95%
what will output index two contain?

00:02:21.435 --> 00:02:26.021 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Do the block count, thread count or selected array indices need to change?

00:02:31.841 --> 00:02:35.681 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Three point zero times zero point five is one point five.

00:02:35.901 --> 00:02:38.205 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The indices and launch stay unchanged.

00:02:38.425 --> 00:02:40.815 line:0 position:50% size:95%
In runKernel in the CUDA source editor,

00:02:41.035 --> 00:02:45.067 line:0 position:50% size:95%
set the multiplier to zero point five f, click Compile and run,

00:02:45.287 --> 00:02:48.444 line:0 position:50% size:95%
and inspect the third value in Console's Output array.
